"""Scene definitions for the Inspecting TLS Traffic with Wireshark teaching video (generated from concept.toml)."""

SCENES = [
    dict(key='01-poster', kind='poster', title='Inspecting TLS Traffic with Wireshark',
         body=None,
         narration="Hello, and welcome. [[slnc 400]] This video explains inspecting T L S, transport layer security, traffic with Wireshark's command line tool, tshark. [[slnc 300]] This video is presented by Jayasekhar Konduru. [[slnc 600]] First, a simple definition. [[slnc 300]] A recording of a real T L S login shows the shop's name, the times, and the sizes, but not the password. [[slnc 300]] With the key log that the client wrote, tshark opens that one connection, and no other. [[slnc 300]] A stolen server key opens only recordings made without one time keys. [[slnc 600]] Think of a sealed envelope. [[slnc 300]] Anyone sees the address and the weight, but only the right letter opener reads it. [[slnc 600]] Our example is an online store."),
    dict(key='02-scenario', kind='bullets', title='The Scenario',
         body=['A shopper signs in to an online store', 'over TLS 1.3.', '', 'The demo runs a real nginx, the openssl client', 'with a key log, and a small tap', 'that records every byte to a capture file.', '', 'tshark reads the file, with and without keys.'],
         narration="Here is the scenario. [[slnc 400]] A shopper signs in to an online store over T L S one point three. [[slnc 500]] The demo runs a real nginx, and the open S S L client, which writes a key log. [[slnc 300]] A small tap, written in Java, sits between them and records every byte to a capture file, so no network card or special rights are needed. [[slnc 500]] Then tshark reads that file, with no keys, with the key log, and with the server's own key."),
    dict(key='03-words', kind='bullets', title='The Words',
         body=['Capture: a file of every packet that passed', "Client Hello: the client's first message, in the clear", 'Client random: 32 new bytes in every Client Hello', 'Key log: the secrets of a connection, written down', 'Key exchange: how both sides get the same secret'],
         narration="Before the demos, the words. [[slnc 500]] A capture is a file of every packet that passed, as a watcher on the path would record it. [[slnc 500]] The client hello is the client's first message, sent before any key exists, so it is in the clear. [[slnc 500]] The client random is thirty two new bytes in every client hello. [[slnc 500]] A key log is a file where the client writes down the secrets of a connection. [[slnc 500]] And the key exchange is how client and server end up with the same secret."),
    dict(key='04-letters-1', kind='bullets', title='What the Letters Stand For',
         body=['TLS: Transport Layer Security: the encrypted, checked connection under https', 'SSL: Secure Sockets Layer: the old name of TLS, kept in names like SSLKEYLOGFILE', 'HTTP: Hypertext Transfer Protocol: the requests and responses of the web', 'pcap: packet capture: the file format of a recording', 'IP, TCP: Internet Protocol and Transmission Control Protocol: the packets under TLS', 'RSA: Rivest, Shamir, Adleman: a public key algorithm'],
         narration='A few letters come up again and again, so here is what they stand for. [[slnc 400]] T L S is transport layer security: the encrypted and checked connection that https runs on. [[slnc 400]] S S L is secure sockets layer, the old name of T L S. It lives on in names like the S S L key log file variable. [[slnc 400]] H T T P is the hypertext transfer protocol: the requests and responses of the web. [[slnc 400]] pcap is short for packet capture: the file format of a recording. [[slnc 400]] I P is the internet protocol, and T C P the transmission control protocol: the packets that carry T L S. [[slnc 400]] R S A is named after Rivest, Shamir and Adleman: a public key algorithm.'),
    dict(key='05-letters-2', kind='bullets', title='What the Letters Stand For (continued)',
         body=['ECDHE: Elliptic Curve Diffie-Hellman Ephemeral: a key exchange with one-time keys', 'AES, GCM: Advanced Encryption Standard, in Galois/Counter Mode, which adds a tag', 'SHA, SHA384: Secure Hash Algorithm: SHA384 gives 384 bits', 'CBC: Cipher Block Chaining: an older AES mode', 'hex: hexadecimal: each byte written as two digits from 0 to f', 'JDK: Java Development Kit'],
         narration='E C D H E is elliptic curve Diffie Hellman ephemeral: a key exchange with one time keys. [[slnc 400]] A E S is the advanced encryption standard. G C M is Galois counter mode, which adds a tag that detects changes. [[slnc 400]] S H A is the secure hash algorithm. S H A three eighty four gives three hundred and eighty four bits. [[slnc 400]] C B C is cipher block chaining, an older mode of A E S. [[slnc 400]] hex is hexadecimal: each byte written as two digits, from zero to f. [[slnc 400]] J D K is the Java development kit.'),
    dict(key='06-wire-and-key-log', kind='figure', title='The same login, two ways',
         body='wire_and_key_log',
         narration="Let's see the whole idea on one picture. [[slnc 400]] At the top, the login the shopper's client sends: a post to slash login, with user ana and password hunter two, inside T L S one point three. [[slnc 700]] On the left, the capture read with no secrets. [[slnc 300]] The server name, in the clear. [[slnc 300]] The cipher the server chose. [[slnc 300]] No certificate, no H T T P, and a login record of one hundred and eighty bytes. [[slnc 700]] On the right, the very same bytes, read with the key log the client wrote. [[slnc 300]] The request, the password, the response, the session cookie, and even the certificate. [[slnc 700]] Every value here comes from the demo's real output. [[slnc 300]] Without the log, a name, a cipher and sizes. [[slnc 300]] With it, everything."),
    dict(key='07-act-1-figure', kind='figure', title='The first record, and the size of the login',
         body='client_hello_bytes',
         narration="First demo: what the wire shows. [[slnc 400]] Before any key exists, the client sends its client hello, and anyone on the path can read it. [[slnc 600]] Here it is, read by hand from the capture's bytes. [[slnc 300]] A record type of twenty two, which means handshake. [[slnc 300]] A message type of one, which means client hello. [[slnc 300]] Thirty two random bytes, new for every connection. [[slnc 300]] Remember them, they come back in the key log. [[slnc 300]] And the server name: w w w dot shop dot localhost, in the clear. [[slnc 700]] Encryption hides the bytes, but not how many there are. [[slnc 300]] The login request is one hundred and sixty three bytes. [[slnc 300]] T L S one point three adds one byte for the content type, and a sixteen byte A E S G C M tag. [[slnc 300]] One hundred and sixty three, plus one, plus sixteen, is one hundred and eighty. [[slnc 400]] And tshark finds a record of exactly one hundred and eighty bytes from the client. [[slnc 300]] A watcher can tell that a login happened."),
    dict(key='08-act-1-1', kind='console', title='Act One — What the wire shows',
         body="ONE. What the wire shows.\n  THE LOGIN (what the client sends, inside TLS 1.3):\n    POST /login  Host: www.shop.localhost\n    user=ana&password=hunter2\n  THE CAPTURE, READ WITH NO SECRETS (tshark -r login.pcap):\n    Client Hello  server name www.shop.localhost\n    Server Hello  TLS_AES_256_GCM_SHA384\n    certificate   not visible\n    HTTP          0 requests readable\n    the word password in the file's bytes: no",
         narration='Here is the real output. [[slnc 400]] The login, as the client sends it inside T L S. [[slnc 400]] Then the capture, read by tshark with no secrets. [[slnc 300]] It sees the name and the cipher. [[slnc 300]] No certificate, no readable request, and the word password is nowhere in the file.'),
    dict(key='09-act-1-2', kind='console', title='Act One — What the wire shows',
         body="ONE. (continued)\n  THE CLIENT HELLO, BY HAND (the first record's bytes):\n    record type   22 (handshake)\n    message type  1 (Client Hello)\n    random        32 bytes, new for every connection\n    server_name   www.shop.localhost  <- IN THE CLEAR\n  THE REQUEST'S SIZE, WORKED OUT (what a watcher still sees):\n    request 163 + type 1 + tag 16 = 180 bytes\n    on the wire (tshark)  180 bytes",
         narration='The client hello, by hand: type twenty two, type one, thirty two random bytes, and the name in the clear. [[slnc 400]] And the size worked out: one hundred and sixty three, plus one, plus sixteen, is one hundred and eighty, which is what tshark sees on the wire.'),
    dict(key='10-act-2-figure', kind='figure', title='One line of a key log, taken apart',
         body='key_log_line',
         narration="Second demo: a key log. [[slnc 400]] The open S S L client was started with dash keylogfile. [[slnc 300]] A browser does the same when the S S L key log file variable is set. [[slnc 700]] Here is one line, taken apart. [[slnc 300]] First a label, which says which secret this is. [[slnc 300]] Then the client random: the same thirty two bytes the client hello carried in the clear. [[slnc 300]] That is how a line names its connection. [[slnc 300]] Then the secret itself. [[slnc 700]] Why forty eight bytes? [[slnc 300]] The server chose T L S A E S two fifty six G C M S H A three eighty four. [[slnc 300]] In T L S one point three, a secret is as long as the suite's hash. [[slnc 300]] S H A three eighty four gives three hundred and eighty four bits, divided by eight, forty eight bytes. [[slnc 700]] At the bottom, the five labels. [[slnc 300]] Each side has a handshake secret, and a traffic secret for its data. [[slnc 300]] The exporter secret opens no traffic at all."),
    dict(key='11-act-2-1', kind='console', title='Act Two — A key log: the secrets of one connection',
         body="TWO. A key log: the secrets of one connection.\n  THE INPUT (openssl s_client -keylogfile keys.log):\n    5 lines, as a browser writes with SSLKEYLOGFILE set\n  THE KEY LOG (each label, and what its secret opens):\n    CLIENT_HANDSHAKE_TRAFFIC_SECRET  the client's handshake\n    SERVER_HANDSHAKE_TRAFFIC_SECRET  the server's handshake\n    CLIENT_TRAFFIC_SECRET_0          the client's data\n    SERVER_TRAFFIC_SECRET_0          the server's data\n    EXPORTER_SECRET                  no traffic",
         narration='The output. [[slnc 400]] Five lines in the key log. [[slnc 300]] Two handshake secrets, two traffic secrets, one for each side, and the exporter secret.'),
    dict(key='12-act-2-2', kind='console', title='Act Two — A key log: the secrets of one connection',
         body="TWO. (continued)\n  ONE LINE, TAKEN APART:\n    label          CLIENT_TRAFFIC_SECRET_0\n    client random  32 bytes (64 hex digits)\n    secret         48 bytes (96 hex digits)\n  THE SECRET'S SIZE, WORKED OUT (from the suite's hash):\n    TLS_AES_256_GCM_SHA384 -> SHA384 -> 384 / 8 = 48 bytes\n  THE MATCH, BY HAND (whose connection is it?):\n    one client random in all lines  yes\n    the Client Hello's random       yes\n    a private key in the file       no",
         narration="One line taken apart: a label, thirty two bytes of client random, and forty eight bytes of secret. [[slnc 400]] Forty eight, because S H A three eighty four divided by eight is forty eight. [[slnc 400]] And the match by hand: one client random in every line, the same as the client hello's. [[slnc 300]] No private key anywhere in the file."),
    dict(key='13-act-3-figure', kind='figure', title='Each secret opens its own part',
         body='opened_by_secret',
         narration="Third demo: the same capture file, read again, now with the key log. [[slnc 400]] Not one byte of the capture has changed. [[slnc 700]] At the top, the request, opened with the client's traffic secret. [[slnc 300]] A post to slash login, user ana, password hunter two. [[slnc 600]] In the middle, the response, opened with the server's traffic secret. [[slnc 300]] Two hundred, welcome ana, and the session cookie. [[slnc 300]] Whoever holds this cookie is logged in as ana. [[slnc 600]] At the bottom, the certificate, opened with the server's handshake secret. [[slnc 300]] In T L S one point three, the certificate travels encrypted. [[slnc 300]] So without the log, a watcher could not even see it. [[slnc 300]] With the log, it is all there: w w w dot shop dot localhost, issued by Shop Lab Root."),
    dict(key='14-act-3', kind='console', title='Act Three — The same capture, read with the key log',
         body='THREE. The same capture, read with the key log.\n  THE INPUT (tshark -o tls.keylog_file:keys.log):\n    the same login.pcap, and the lines of keys.log\n  THE REQUEST (opened with CLIENT_TRAFFIC_SECRET_0):\n    POST /login\n    user     = ana\n    password = hunter2\n  THE RESPONSE (opened with SERVER_TRAFFIC_SECRET_0):\n    200  welcome ana\n    Set-Cookie: session=7f3a; Secure; HttpOnly\n  THE CERTIFICATE (opened with SERVER_HANDSHAKE_TRAFFIC_SECRET):\n    for        www.shop.localhost  <- ENCRYPTED IN TLS 1.3\n    issued by  Shop Lab Root',
         narration='The output. [[slnc 400]] The request opens: a post to slash login, with ana and hunter two. [[slnc 400]] The response opens, with the session cookie. [[slnc 400]] And the encrypted certificate opens too, for w w w dot shop dot localhost, issued by Shop Lab Root.'),
    dict(key='15-act-4-figure', kind='figure', title='A log matches by client random',
         body='one_connection',
         narration="Fourth demo: a key log opens its own connection, and no other. [[slnc 400]] The shopper logs in a second time, and the client writes a second key log. [[slnc 700]] On each row, a log, and the question asked by hand: does it carry the second login's client random? [[slnc 300]] The first log does not. [[slnc 300]] tshark agrees: zero H T T P packets, closed. [[slnc 300]] The second log does, and it opens. [[slnc 300]] Both logs in one file carry it, and open both logins. [[slnc 700]] At the bottom, half a log. [[slnc 300]] Before, with all five lines, requests and responses open. [[slnc 300]] After, with only the client's two lines, the requests open, and the responses stay closed. [[slnc 300]] Each side encrypts with its own secret. [[slnc 300]] A key log opens exactly what it lists."),
    dict(key='16-act-4-1', kind='console', title='Act Four — A key log opens its own connection and no other',
         body="FOUR. A key log opens its own connection and no other.\n  THE INPUT (a second login, with its own key log):\n    login.pcap   keys.log    the first login\n    login2.pcap  keys2.log   the second login\n  THE MATCH, BY HAND (the log has login2's client random?):\n    keys.log   no\n    keys2.log  yes\n    both.log   yes  (keys.log and keys2.log in one file)",
         narration="The output. [[slnc 400]] The two logins and their logs. [[slnc 400]] By hand, only the second log, and the joined file, carry the second login's client random."),
    dict(key='17-act-4-2', kind='console', title='Act Four — A key log opens its own connection and no other',
         body='FOUR. (continued)\n  THE RESULT (tshark on login2.pcap):\n    keys.log   closed: 0 HTTP packets\n    keys2.log  opened\n    both.log   opened, and login.pcap: opened\n  BEFORE (all the lines of keys.log, on login.pcap):\n    requests opened, responses opened\n  AFTER (only its two CLIENT_ lines, on login.pcap):\n    requests opened, responses closed  <- ONE SECRET PER SIDE',
         narration="And tshark agrees: the first log opens nothing of the second login, its own log opens it, the joined file opens both. [[slnc 400]] With only the client's lines, the responses stay closed."),
    dict(key='18-act-5-figure', kind='figure', title='Ephemeral keys, or a secret that travels',
         body='key_exchange',
         narration="Fifth demo: the server's own private key. [[slnc 400]] Suppose it leaks, a year after a watcher recorded the traffic. [[slnc 700]] On the left, nginx over T L S one point two, with E C D H E. [[slnc 300]] Each side makes a one time key, and the server's key only signs its part. [[slnc 300]] Each side works out the secret, and the secret never crosses the wire. [[slnc 300]] So the leaked key opens nothing: zero H T T P packets. [[slnc 700]] On the right, an old style server with R S A key exchange. [[slnc 300]] The client picks the secret, locks it with the server's public key, and sends it. [[slnc 300]] The secret is in the recording, locked. [[slnc 300]] And the leaked private key unlocks it. [[slnc 300]] tshark opens the login, password hunter two. [[slnc 700]] That is forward secrecy. [[slnc 300]] T L S one point three always uses one time keys, so it has no R S A key exchange at all."),
    dict(key='19-act-5-1', kind='console', title="Act Five — The server's own key is a different matter",
         body="FIVE. The server's own key is a different matter.\n  THE RECORDINGS (the suite each Server Hello chose):\n    TLS 1.3, nginx  TLS_AES_256_GCM_SHA384\n    TLS 1.2, nginx  TLS_ECDHE_RSA_WITH_AES_256_GCM_SHA384\n    TLS 1.2, old    TLS_RSA_WITH_AES_128_CBC_SHA\n  THE CERTIFICATE, WITH NO SECRETS:\n    TLS 1.3  not visible\n    TLS 1.2  visible",
         narration='The output. [[slnc 400]] Three recordings, and the suite each server chose. [[slnc 400]] Over T L S one point two, the certificate is visible, with no secret. [[slnc 300]] Over one point three, it is not.'),
    dict(key='20-act-5-2', kind='console', title="Act Five — The server's own key is a different matter",
         body="FIVE. (continued)\n  THE KEY EXCHANGE, BY HAND (read from the suite's name):\n    TLS 1.3, nginx  ephemeral: a new key pair per connection\n    TLS 1.2, nginx  ephemeral (ECDHE): the server key only signs\n    TLS 1.2, old    RSA: the secret is sent locked to the server key\n  THE RULE, BY HAND (did the secret cross the wire?):\n    TLS 1.2, nginx  no, so the key opens nothing\n    TLS 1.2, old    yes, so the key opens it\n  THE LEAK (tshark with the server's private key, rsa.key):\n    TLS 1.2, nginx  closed: 0 HTTP packets\n    TLS 1.2, old    opened: password hunter2  <- THE PAST IS OPEN",
         narration='The key exchange, read by hand from the name. [[slnc 400]] Only the R S A one sends the secret across the wire, so only it should open. [[slnc 400]] And tshark, with the leaked key, agrees: the E C D H E recording stays closed, the R S A recording opens, password and all.'),
    dict(key='21-costs', kind='bullets', title='What It Costs',
         body=['A key log opens every connection it lists', 'Names, times and sizes stay visible', 'Capturing a real network needs rights', 'Decryption tempts teams to leave logging on'],
         narration='Nothing is free. [[slnc 400]] Here is what it costs. [[slnc 500]] A key log opens every connection it lists, so it is as sensitive as the passwords inside them. [[slnc 500]] Encryption hides the content, but the names, the times and the sizes stay visible. [[slnc 500]] Recording a real network needs rights that a test should not ask for. [[slnc 300]] And once decryption makes debugging easy, a team is tempted to leave key logging on.'),
    dict(key='22-recognise', kind='bullets', title='Where You Have Seen It',
         body=['SSLKEYLOGFILE in an environment', 'Wireshark showing only "Application Data"', 'A capture file attached to a support ticket', '"(Pre)-Master-Secret log filename" in Wireshark'],
         narration='You have probably met this already. [[slnc 400]] An S S L key log file variable, in an environment, is a client writing its secrets down. [[slnc 500]] A Wireshark screen full of application data records is a capture without keys. [[slnc 500]] A capture file attached to a support ticket is sensitive, even when it is encrypted. [[slnc 500]] And the pre master secret log setting in Wireshark is where the key log is read.'),
    dict(key='23-verdict', kind='bullets', title='How To Use It',
         body=['Keep key logs out of production; delete them', 'Ephemeral key exchange only', 'Names, times and sizes stay visible', 'Treat every capture as sensitive'],
         narration='So, how do you use it? [[slnc 400]] Keep key logs out of production, and delete them after use. [[slnc 500]] Offer only ephemeral key exchange, so a leaked server key opens nothing that was recorded. [[slnc 500]] Remember that names, times, and sizes stay visible to anyone on the path. [[slnc 300]] And treat every capture as sensitive, even when it is encrypted.'),
    dict(key='24-classes', kind='bullets', title='The Java Classes',
         body=['ClientHello: reads the first record by hand, as anyone on the path can', 'KeyLog: the key log file, and which connection a line opens', 'Suites: what a suite name says about the key exchange', 'Tap: forwards every byte and writes it to a capture file', 'Tshark: runs tshark on a capture, with or without secrets', 'Client: the openssl client with a key log, and a JDK client for RSA'],
         narration='Now, a quick look at the code. [[slnc 300]] Three small classes redo by hand what tshark decides: the client hello, the key log match, and the key exchange. [[slnc 300]] Around them, a tap that writes the capture, and the real tools. [[slnc 300]] Here they are.'),
    dict(key='25-class-1', kind='code', title='ClientHello — the random and the server name',
         body='// ClientHello: reads the first record by hand, as anyone on the path can\nint recordType = b.get() & 0xff;\n...\nbyte[] random = new byte[32];\nb.get(random);\n...\nif (type == SERVER_NAME) {\n...\nname = new String(host, StandardCharsets.US_ASCII);',
         narration='It walks the bytes of the first record: the record type, the message type, then thirty two bytes of random. [[slnc 300]] It skips the session, the suites and the compression, then reads the extensions until it finds the server name. [[slnc 300]] A test proves it gives the same random and name as tshark.'),
    dict(key='26-class-2', kind='code', title='KeyLog — a log names connections by client random',
         body='// KeyLog: the key log file, and which connection a line opens\nboolean opens(String clientRandom) {\n    return lines.stream()\n            .anyMatch(l -> l.clientRandom().equalsIgnoreCase(clientRandom));\n}\n...\nKeyLog only(String prefix) {',
         narration="Each line is a label, a client random and a secret. [[slnc 300]] A log opens a capture only when one of its lines carries that capture's client random. [[slnc 300]] Only keeps the lines of one side, for the half log of the fourth demo."),
    dict(key='27-class-3', kind='code', title='Suites — ephemeral, or RSA',
         body='// Suites: what a suite name says about the key exchange\nif (!name.contains("_WITH_")) {\n    return "ephemeral: a new key pair per connection";\n}\n...\nstatic boolean serverKeyOpens(String name) {\n    return name.startsWith("TLS_RSA_WITH_");\n}\n...\nreturn Integer.parseInt(hash(name).substring(3)) / 8;',
         narration='The name of a T L S one point three suite has no key exchange in it, because it is always ephemeral. [[slnc 300]] A server key opens a recording only for names that start with T L S R S A with. [[slnc 300]] And a secret is as long as the hash: three eighty four bits divided by eight.'),
    dict(key='28-class-4', kind='code', title='Tap — copy, then forward',
         body='// Tap: forwards every byte and writes it to a capture file\nwhile ((n = in.read(buffer)) > 0) {\n    pcap.data(fromClient, buffer, n);\n    out.write(buffer, 0, n);',
         narration='The tap sits between the client and the server on loopback. [[slnc 300]] Every chunk it reads is written to the capture, then passed on. [[slnc 300]] Pcap wraps the chunks in I P and T C P headers, so tshark reads it like any recording.'),
    dict(key='29-class-5', kind='code', title="Tshark — a key log, or the server's key",
         body='// Tshark: runs tshark on a capture, with or without secrets\nstatic List<String> keyLog(Path keys) {\n    return List.of("tls.keylog_file:" + keys);\n}\n...\nreturn List.of("uat:rsa_keys:\\"" + key + "\\",\\"\\"");',
         narration="tshark is given one extra setting. [[slnc 300]] The key log file, for the third and fourth demos. [[slnc 300]] Or the server's private key, for the fifth."),
    dict(key='30-class-6', kind='code', title='Client — write the secrets, or offer only RSA',
         body='// Client: the openssl client with a key log, and a JDK client for RSA\ncommand.addAll(List.of("-keylogfile", keyLog.toString()));\n...\nString[] rsaOnly = {"TLS_RSA_WITH_AES_128_CBC_SHA"};\nsocket.setEnabledCipherSuites(rsaOnly);',
         narration="The open S S L client writes its secrets when it is given dash keylogfile. [[slnc 300]] The second client is the J D K's, told to offer only R S A key exchange, for the old style server."),
    dict(key='31-outro', kind='outro', title='Thanks for Watching',
         body=['Full source, notes, diagrams and an animated walkthrough', 'are in the repository. Add a third login and a key', 'log with only its server lines.'],
         narration="That's inspecting T L S traffic with Wireshark. [[slnc 400]] If you remember one sentence, make it this one. [[slnc 300]] Encryption hides the content and not the conversation, and the secrets that open it are kept per connection, by whoever wrote them down. [[slnc 500]] The full source code, written notes, diagrams, and an animated walkthrough are all in the repository. [[slnc 300]] It runs on your own machine. [[slnc 500]] Here is one exercise to try. [[slnc 300]] Add a third login, and a key log with only its server lines, and see which half opens. [[slnc 500]] If you found this useful, a like really helps other people discover it. [[slnc 300]] Kindly subscribe to the channel. [[slnc 300]] It helps the channel grow and bring you more content. [[slnc 400]] Thanks for watching!"),
]

